Multi-model side-by-side mode — load YOLO26n and the fine-tuned MobileNetV2 ELU at the same time and display both decisions on the overlay. Demonstrates your best two models simultaneously and lets you answer "why YOLO over MobileNet?" live.

Session evidence log — auto-write a demo_session_<timestamp>.json + demo_session_<timestamp>.md containing every decision (timestamp, model, class, confidence, latency, capture path). At end-of-session, print a summary table (total frames, PASS count, FAIL count, mean latency, mean confidence). This gives you the "saved examples of live PASS/FAIL decisions" the rubric wants.

Hotkeys for the demo flow:

SPACE — capture & log a frame manually (good for the moment when you hold up an unseen cup)
g then 1/2 — assert ground truth (PASS/FAIL) → running accuracy displayed
p — pause inference
q — quit + write summary
Medium-impact
Better overlay — probability bar for both classes, FPS counter, running PASS/FAIL counts, model name. Currently shows only top-1 confidence.

Confidence threshold flag — --confidence-threshold 0.6 so low-confidence frames show "UNCERTAIN" rather than forcing a binary call. Useful talking point on model limitations.

Auto-detect model type — handle both *.pt YOLO weights and PyTorch state_dicts (your MobileNetV2 checkpoints) without separate scripts.

Graceful missing-model handling — current script crashes hard. Print available models in runs/ and exit cleanly.

Workflow integrity
Choose one canonical dataset state and freeze it for the final submission. Right now the repo mixes the balanced `defect/non_defect` experiment snapshot with the later imbalanced `defective/non_defective` processed split, which makes the final story harder to defend.

Add a dataset lineage manifest that records: raw capture count, augmentation config hash, cleanup settings, balancing step, split seed, and final split counts. This should make it obvious which run folders came from which dataset state.

Make the end-to-end workflow reproducible with one documented source-of-truth command sequence or one orchestration script. At the moment the scaffold workflow, the experiment stack, and the live demo path are split across multiple directories.

Replace the broken machine-specific data linkage in `defect_classification_stack` with a repo-relative path strategy or an explicit setup step. The current macOS absolute symlink is not portable.

Dataset quality
Reduce dependence on the 360-degree deterministic rotation sweep as the primary augmentation strategy. It creates volume, but it is too synthetic and too correlated to substitute for more real PASS samples.

Add a pre-training dataset summary artifact that saves class counts, source-group counts, and duplicate-removal statistics into one report. This should be generated automatically after cleanup/balancing/splitting.

Add a check that prevents training on an obviously imbalanced `data/processed` split unless the imbalance is intentional and acknowledged. This would have caught the later rerun condition earlier.

Persist a small held-out demo set manifest for the five genuinely unseen live-demo samples so they are not accidentally reused during training or augmentation.

Metrics and reporting
Fix the positive-class ambiguity across the experiment stack. Several scripts infer class order alphabetically and then compute binary precision/recall/F1, which can silently make `non_defect` the positive class instead of `defect`.

Save a full YOLO test evaluation bundle: confusion matrix, classification report, per-image predictions CSV, and explicit PASS/FAIL mapping. Do not rely only on top-1/top-5 for the final comparison.

Separate "headline accuracy" from "engineering safety metric" in the final tables. For the assessment, defect recall / false-PASS rate needs to be visible, not just aggregate accuracy or top-1.

Create one final report table with consistent metric semantics across all model families. Do not mirror YOLO top-1 into an F1 column in the aggregate ranking.

Demo readiness
Unify the demo entry point. Decide whether `demo_live_test.py` or `infer_yolo26_cls_stream.py` is the final script and document one exact command for the assessment day.

Move stream credentials out of commands and source files into environment variables or a local config that is excluded from git. This reduces risk and makes the demo safer to run on another machine.

Add an offline demo mode that runs the same overlay and logging flow on a folder of unseen test images or a local video file. That gives a reliable fallback if the live stream fails.

Add explicit setup-status overlays during demo startup: stream connected, auth mode used, model loaded, class names detected, and current frame size. That will make technical questioning easier to handle live.
